<section class="docs-section" id="edm">
        <h2>@Localizer["Wire EDM and sinker EDM: different access problems"]</h2>
        <p>@Localizer["Electrical discharge machining removes electrically conductive material through spark erosion. It can be considered for hard materials and details that are difficult to produce with rotating cutters. Wire EDM transports a wire electrode between guides; offsetting the guides can create tapered cuts. Sinker EDM uses a shaped electrode that advances into the workpiece. These tool arrangements create different geometry and access requirements."]</p>
        <p>@Localizer["As a geometric consequence, a through-profile with a usable wire path is a natural candidate for wire EDM, while a blind cavity can require evaluation of sinker EDM. Review threading access, retained support, flushing and finish requirements. Neither process means perfectly sharp internal corners or freedom from distortion. Ask whether milling plus a specialist finishing operation is more appropriate than routing the entire part through EDM; confirm availability and responsibility in the quotation."]</p>
        <aside class="docs-example">@Localizer["Illustrative example: a closed rectangular opening through a conductive plate needs a wire-entry plan and a permitted corner radius. A similar outline stopping inside the plate is a blind cavity, so it presents a different electrode and access problem. These are process-selection examples, not service commitments."]</aside>
        <p class="docs-source"><a href="https://www.makino.com/en-us/machine-technology/machining-process/edm">@Localizer["Makino: spark erosion and shaped sinker electrodes"]</a></p>
        <p class="docs-source"><a href="https://www.makino.com/resources/content-library/article/archive/edm-wire-tutorial/198">@Localizer["Makino: wire guides, taper and machining considerations"]</a></p>
    </section>
<section class="docs-section" id="grinding">
        <h2>@Localizer["Grinding: specify the surface that needs it"]</h2>
        <p>@Localizer["Grinding can be considered when selected surfaces need demanding geometric control or surface texture. Distinguish surface roughness from flatness, parallelism, roundness or size: a low roughness value alone does not define those properties. Name the functional surface and its requirements rather than requesting precision grinding everywhere. The route must account for material condition, access, stock allowance and the sequence of any heat treatment."]</p>
        <p>@Localizer["Norton identifies wheel dressing, grit, coolant, operating parameters and machine condition as contributors to finish. Parameter changes can also introduce thermal damage, so finer appearance should not be treated as proof of an improved process. Ask whether machining alone meets the requirement, which faces need a later operation and how those faces will be inspected. Availability of grinding and any specialist inspection should be confirmed, not inferred from the design guide."]</p>
        <aside class="docs-example">@Localizer["Illustrative example: a sliding face might require Ra 0.8 µm together with a separate flatness requirement, while a non-mating outside face has a different finish. These are drawing choices to validate against the application, not guaranteed results for a particular material or process."]</aside>
        <p class="docs-source"><a href="https://www.nortonabrasives.com/en-us/resources/expertise/5-grinding-considerations-improving-surface-finish">@Localizer["Norton Abrasives: grinding finish and thermal tradeoffs"]</a></p>
    </section>
